  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nWednesday, a strong 597-dam 500-mb upper-level ridge will remain \nanchored across the southwestern United States. Increasing warm air \nadvection (WAA) will support 850-mb temperatures rising into the 25 \nto 30\u00b0C range. Given sufficient daytime mixing, these temperatures \nwould support highs in the middle 90s across much of the area, with \na few locations potentially reaching the lower 100s. As the ridge \nshifts slightly southwestward, the forecast area will transition \ninto more of a northwest-flow (ring of fire) pattern. This pattern \ntypically favors weak disturbances tracking along the northern \nperiphery of the ridge, resulting in periodic chances for showers \nand thunderstorms. Recent model guidance has begun to depict \nconvection developing across Wyoming and western Nebraska before \nmoving southeast toward the forecast area, which is a common \nevolution in this pattern. At this time, the severe weather threat \nappears low, as the strongest deep-layer shear is forecast to remain \nnorth of the area. However, precipitation chances and any associated \nsevere weather potential will continue to be monitored over the next \nseveral forecast cycles. Overnight lows will remain mild, generally \nin the middle 60s.\n\nThursday, northwest flow aloft will remain in place as the upper-\nlevel ridge stays centered over Colorado. High temperatures will \nonce again climb into the middle 90s across much of the area. Some \nmodel guidance continues to suggest a weak shortwave disturbance \nmoving southeast through the flow, which would provide another \nopportunity for showers and thunderstorms. Confidence in the timing \nand coverage of any precipitation remains low at this range. \nOvernight lows will once again remain mild, generally in the middle \n60s.\n\nFriday through the weekend, the upper-level ridge is expected to \nremain amplified across the western United States, maintaining \nnorthwest flow aloft across the central Plains. Temperatures are \nexpected to remain well above normal, with highs generally in the \nmiddle 90s to near 100 degrees as the primary storm track remains \nnorth of the region. Periodic chances for showers and thunderstorms \nwill continue as additional weak disturbances move through the \nnorthwest flow. While these disturbances should provide occasional \nopportunities for precipitation, widespread wetting rainfall does \nnot appear likely at this time. Forecast confidence in the timing \nand placement of these disturbances remains low and will continue to \nbe refined over the coming forecast cycles.",
